    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d522">

                <pb facs="1132" n="1036" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg1036"/>

                <head>No. 489.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fish to Mr. Hunter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate at Tunis</hi></placeName>,<lb/><date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1878-11-26"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 26, 1878</date>. (Received, December 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 15.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose to the State Department copy of a
                    communication to his excellency the prime minister and minister of foreign
                    affairs, embracing the views of the State and Treasury Departments of the United
                    States Government on the subject of a tax proposed to be levied by this
                    government for the maintenance (in part) of light-houses on the coast. The
                    communication embraces the views of the Secretary of State, based on a letter
                    from Rear-Admiral John Rodgers, chairman of Light-House Board, and Hon. John B.
                    Hawley, Acting Secretary of the Treasury, all referred to in letter of the Hon.
                    William M. Evarts, dated October 23, 1878.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEO. W. FISH,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States Consul,
                            Tunis.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
